 { Mauricio Flores Rios – Rodrigo Vasquez, Reno (rapid) 2007 Learning objective: This game is an example of the sharp complications that may arise after Black’s ...d6-d5 break. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e6 7. f3 b5 8. g4 { The purpose of this early g2-g4 } ( { instead of } 8. Qd2 ) 
 { is to provoke: } h6 { This move can be weakening in some cases. } 9. Qd2 Nbd7 10. O-O-O { My database has over two hundred GM-level games in this position. Black decides to go for the sharp continuation: } b4 11. Na4 d5 $5 { Achieving the standard ...d6-d5 break, but while Black’s king is still in the centre. } ( { PH: For context, this opening line was considered dubious for Black for a while until Topalov came up with the nice pawn sacrifice } 11... Ne5 $1 12. Qxb4 Bd7 { White has some problems to solve. If the queen leaves, the a4-knight is stranded, while if the knight returns to c3 immediately, the queen can't really move away from the queenside. } ) 
12. exd5 Nxd5 13. Bc4 $1 { White is playing aggressively and maximum precision is required. If Black manages to stabilize his position and castle, then he is probably going to take the lead. } Bb7 14. Rhe1 Qa5 $2 { An overly optimistic move. } ( { The careless } 14... Nxe3 $2 { loses after } 15. Nxe6 $1 fxe6 16. Bxe6 Bc6 17. Qxe3 { . } ) 
 ( { Safer was } 14... N7f6 15. g5 hxg5 16. Bxg5 { though after } Be7 { as in Negi – David, Belgium 2012, then } 17. Nxe6 $1 fxe6 18. Rxe6 { gives White a promising position since } O-O { is met by } 19. Qe2 $1 Rf7 20. Bxf6 Bxf6 21. Nb6 { winning a decisive amount of material. } ) 
 ( { PH: Black keeps a defensible position with } 14... Be7 15. Nf5 O-O $1 16. Nxe7 Qxe7 17. Bxh6 { and now the cold-blooded } Qd8 $16 { . } ) 
15. Nxe6 $3 { +- This was only a twenty-minute game and I could not calculate much; all I knew was that the nature of the position called for an aggressive move like this one, preserving the initiative. } ( { If } 15. Bb3 $2 O-O-O { ∞ Black is comfortable and White no longer has clear targets to attack. } ) 
fxe6 16. Bb6 $1 { Attacking e6 while covering the d8-square, thus preventing Black’s king from escaping with ...O-O-O. } ( { So of course not } 16. Bf2 $2 O-O-O { . } ) 
Qxa4 ( { White is also winning after: } 16... N7xb6 17. Rxe6 Be7 ( { or } 17... Kd7 18. Nxb6 Kxe6 19. Nxd5 Kf7 18. Rxb6 $18 ) 
18. Nxb6 O-O ( { or } 18... Nxb6 19. Rxe7 $1 Kxe7 20. Qd6 Ke8 21. Qe6 Kf8 22. Qf7 ) 
19. Rxe7 Qxb6 20. Rxb7 $1 ) 
 ( 16... N5xb6 17. Rxe6 Be7 18. Nxb6 { transposes to the variation above. } ) 
17. Bxd5 Be7 ( 17... Nxb6 { is met decisively by: } 18. Rxe6 Be7 19. Rxe7 $1 Kxe7 20. Qe3 Kd7 21. Qxb6 Bxd5 22. Rxd5 ) 
18. Rxe6 Bxd5 ( { Again } 18... Nxb6 { loses to } 19. Rxe7 $1 { as above. } ) 
19. Qxd5 Nxb6 ( { If } 19... Rc8 20. Rxe7 $1 Kxe7 21. Re1 { White has a forced mate, for example: } Kf8 22. Bc5 Nxc5 23. Qf5 Kg8 24. Qxc8 Kh7 25. Qf5 Kg8 26. Re7 $1 ) 
20. Rxb6 Rd8 21. Qh5+ Kf8 22. Qf5+ Kg8 ( { Or } 22... Ke8 23. Qg6 $1 Kf8 24. Rxd8 Bxd8 25. Rb7 { forcing mate. } ) 
23. Rxd8+ Bxd8 24. Qd5+ Kh7 25. Qd3+ ( { Quicker and nicer was: } 25. Rxh6 $1 Kxh6 ( 25. Qd3+ gxh6 26. Qf7 ) 
26. Qh5 ) 
 ( { After } 25. Qd3 { Black resigned because he is losing a rook after } Kg8 26. Qxd8 Kh7 27. Qd3 Kg8 28. Rb8 Kf7 29. Rxh8 { . Final remarks It is dangerous for Black to carry out the ...d6-d5 break before castling. The resulting position is rather complicated, but seems favourable to White as long as he is ready to sacrifice material when appropriate. } ) 

 1-0

